What Java code can I use to calculate the value of H^2 in the Big Bang Theory using the given formula: $H^2=\frac{8 \pi G}{3} \rho -\frac{k}{R^2}+\frac{\Lambda}{3}$? Can the code handle variable inputs for G, rho, k, R, and Lambda without causing any runtime errors? 
Here's an example Java code that can calculate H^2 using the provided formula:
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.MathContext;
public class HCalculation {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BigDecimal G = new BigDecimal("6.67408e-11"); // gravitational constant
        BigDecimal rho = new BigDecimal("2.3e-26"); // density
        BigDecimal k = new BigDecimal("0"); // curvature constant
        BigDecimal R = new BigDecimal("4.4e26"); // radius of the observable universe
        BigDecimal Lambda = new BigDecimal("0"); // cosmological constant

        BigDecimal pi = new BigDecimal(Math.PI);
        BigDecimal three = new BigDecimal(3);
        BigDecimal eight = new BigDecimal(8);

        BigDecimal firstTerm = eight.multiply(pi).multiply(G).multiply(rho).divide(three, MathContext.DECIMAL128);
        BigDecimal secondTerm = k.divide(R.multiply(R), MathContext.DECIMAL128);
        BigDecimal thirdTerm = Lambda.divide(three, MathContext.DECIMAL128);

        BigDecimal result = firstTerm.subtract(secondTerm).add(thirdTerm);

        System.out.println("H^2 = " + result.toString());
    }
}
This code initializes the variables G, rho, k, R, and Lambda as BigDecimal objects to handle variable inputs. It then calculates each term in the formula separately and adds them up to get the final result, which is printed to the console.

