Yes, I can help you with that. One of the simplest sorting algorithms is the bubble sort algorithm. Here's how you can implement it in Java:
```
public static void bubbleSort(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (nums[j] > nums[j + 1]) {
                int temp = nums[j];
                nums[j] = nums[j + 1];
                nums[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this algorithm, we iterate over the array and compare adjacent elements. If the element on the left is greater than the element on the right, we swap them. We repeat this process until the array is sorted.
To use this algorithm to sort the list of integers you provided, you can simply call the `bubbleSort` method and pass in the `nums` array:
```
int[] nums = {5, 2, 9, 1, 3, 8};
bubbleSort(nums);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));
```
This will output: `[1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9]`

