Yes, I can give you a possible solution to this puzzle. One approach to ensuring data privacy in a Go application without using AES or RSA encryption methods is by using a one-time pad encryption technique. This technique involves generating a random key of the same length as the plaintext and XORing the plaintext with the key to produce the ciphertext. The key is then securely exchanged between the sender and the receiver, and the receiver can use the key to decrypt the ciphertext.
Here's an example Go code snippet that implements the one-time pad encryption technique:
```go
package main
import (
	"crypto/rand"
	"fmt"
)
func main() {
	// Generate a random key of the same length as the plaintext
	key := make([]byte, 10)
	_, err := rand.Read(key)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	// Plaintext to be encrypted
	plaintext := []byte("Hello, world!")
	// Encrypt the plaintext using the one-time pad technique
	ciphertext := make([]byte, len(plaintext))
	for i := 0; i < len(plaintext); i++ {
		ciphertext[i] = plaintext[i] ^ key[i]
	}
	// Decrypt the ciphertext using the key
	decrypted := make([]byte, len(ciphertext))
	for i := 0; i < len(ciphertext); i++ {
		decrypted[i] = ciphertext[i] ^ key[i]
	}
	fmt.Printf("Plaintext: %s\n", plaintext)
	fmt.Printf("Ciphertext: %x\n", ciphertext)
	fmt.Printf("Decrypted: %s\n", decrypted)
}
```
In this code snippet, a random key of length 10 is generated using the `crypto/rand` package. The plaintext is then encrypted using the one-time pad technique by XORing each byte of the plaintext with the corresponding byte of the key. The ciphertext is printed in hexadecimal format. The ciphertext can be securely exchanged between the sender and the receiver, and the receiver can use the key to decrypt the ciphertext by XORing each byte of the ciphertext with the corresponding byte of the key. The decrypted plaintext is printed to verify that the encryption and decryption worked correctly.
Note that the one-time pad technique is only secure if the key is truly random, used only once, and kept secret. If the key is reused or leaked, the security of the encryption is compromised.

