To sort a list of numbers in Python with the added constraint that even numbers appear before odd numbers while maintaining their respective order within each category, we can use a modified merge sort algorithm. In this algorithm, we first divide the list into two halves and recursively sort each half. Once the two halves are sorted, we merge them by comparing the first element of each half and placing the smaller one into a new list. We continue this process until we have merged all the elements into the new list.
To implement the added constraint, we can modify the merge step to only compare and merge elements of the same parity. Specifically, we can maintain two pointers, one for even elements and one for odd elements, and merge the even elements first followed by the odd elements. Within each category, we can use the standard comparison operator to maintain the original order.
Here is the modified merge sort algorithm in Python:
```
def merge_sort(arr):
    if len(arr) > 1:
        mid = len(arr) // 2
        left_half = arr[:mid]
        right_half = arr[mid:]
        merge_sort(left_half)
        merge_sort(right_half)
        i = j = k = 0
        while i < len(left_half) and j < len(right_half):
            if left_half[i] % 2 == 0 and right_half[j] % 2 == 0:
                if left_half[i] <= right_half[j]:
                    arr[k] = left_half[i]
                    i += 1
                else:
                    arr[k] = right_half[j]
                    j += 1
            elif left_half[i] % 2 == 0:
                arr[k] = left_half[i]
                i += 1
            elif right_half[j] % 2 == 0:
                arr[k] = right_half[j]
                j += 1
            else:
                if left_half[i] <= right_half[j]:
                    arr[k] = left_half[i]
                    i += 1
                else:
                    arr[k] = right_half[j]
                    j += 1
            k += 1
        while i < len(left_half):
            if left_half[i] % 2 == 0:
                arr[k] = left_half[i]
                k += 1
            i += 1
        while j < len(right_half):
            if right_half[j] % 2 == 0:
                arr[k] = right_half[j]
                k += 1
            j += 1
```
In this implementation, we first check if an element is even before comparing it to another element. If both elements are even, we use the standard comparison operator to maintain their original order. If one element is even and the other is odd, we place the even element first. If both elements are odd, we again use the standard comparison operator to maintain their original order. After merging the two halves, we check for any remaining even or odd elements and place them at the end of the list.

